
<% content_for :title, "The Big Picture & Bipolar | Bipolar Sober" %>

<style>
  .big-picture-page .big-picture-copy,
  .big-picture-page .big-picture-stack > div {
    min-width: 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
</style>

<main class="big-picture-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="big-picture-hero">
    <div class="big-picture-hero-content">

      <p class="big-picture-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / BIPOLAR / PERSPECTIVES / THE BIG PICTURE
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>THE</span>
        <span>BIG</span>
        <span>PICTURE.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="big-picture-intro">
        DIFFERENT LENSES.<br>
        SAME COMPLICATED FUCKING HUMAN.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / INTRODUCTION -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-pink"
           aria-labelledby="big-picture-section-1-title">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">

      <p class="big-picture-number">
        01 / SO WHAT'S THE POINT?
      </p>

      <h2 id="big-picture-section-1-title">
        NOBODY<br>
        GETS THE<br>
        WHOLE THING.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p>
        When you live with bipolar disorder, you get plenty
        of chances to question what the fuck your brain is
        doing. Buddhism, Hinduism, Stoicism, CBT, DBT, IFS,
        Taoism, Paganism, and Shinto offer different ways
        to understand your experience.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some emphasize awareness and acceptance. Others
        focus on responsibility, identity, relationships,
        or living with change. They don't all agree,
        and they don't have to. Each perspective gives
        you another way to examine your life without
        reducing everything to your diagnosis.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / BUDDHISM -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-yellow"
           aria-labelledby="big-picture-section-2-title">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">

      <p class="big-picture-number">
        02 / BUDDHISM
      </p>

      <h2 id="big-picture-section-2-title">
        THIS WILL<br>
        CHANGE.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p>
        Buddhism begins by acknowledging dukkha:
        suffering and dissatisfaction. Bipolar disorder
        brings pain you did not choose. The image of
        two arrows distinguishes that pain from the
        extra suffering shame, self-hatred, or
        resistance can add.
      </p>

      <p>
        Impermanence reminds you that thoughts, emotions,
        and circumstances change. A lifelong diagnosis
        doesn't mean every day will feel the same.
        Nonself invites you to loosen fixed labels
        instead of making one episode your whole identity.
      </p>

      <p>
        Mindfulness helps you notice what is happening
        before reacting when possible. The Eightfold
        Path connects awareness with compassion and
        ethical action. The Middle Way encourages
        acceptance without resignation.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can acknowledge suffering, take responsibility
        for your choices, and continue practicing
        without demanding perfection.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / HINDUISM -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-purple"
           aria-labelledby="big-picture-section-3-title">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">

      <p class="big-picture-number">
        03 / HINDUISM
      </p>

      <h2 id="big-picture-section-3-title">
        YOU ARE<br>
        MORE THAN<br>
        THIS STATE.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p>
        Some Hindu traditions approach identity through
        Atman, the enduring Self beyond changing mental
        states. This differs from Buddhist nonself,
        but offers another perspective on worth
        beyond symptoms.
      </p>

      <p>
        The gunas provide philosophical language for
        changing qualities of experience, while
        dharma encourages responsible action.
        You can recognize that your mind is changing
        without assuming that every mood reflects
        your deepest identity.
      </p>

      <p>
        You aren't defined by mania, depression,
        or the consequences of an episode.
        Your responsibilities still matter,
        and your worth isn't determined by
        your current state of mind.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / STOICISM -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-black"
           aria-labelledby="big-picture-section-4-title">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">

      <p class="big-picture-number">
        04 / STOICISM
      </p>

      <h2 id="big-picture-section-4-title">
        WHAT'S<br>
        ACTUALLY<br>
        MINE?
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p>
        Stoicism asks what is yours to influence.
        You cannot undo the past, prevent every symptom,
        or guarantee another episode will never happen.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can prepare, follow treatment, establish
        safeguards, and seek support. When something
        goes wrong, you can focus on your response
        rather than spending your energy wishing
        reality were different.
      </p>

      <p>
        A setback can become a problem to work with,
        rather than proof that all your progress
        has disappeared.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / CBT -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-pink"
           aria-labelledby="big-picture-section-5-title">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">

      <p class="big-picture-number">
        05 / CBT
      </p>

      <h2 id="big-picture-section-5-title">
        CHECK THE<br>
        FUCKING<br>
        RECEIPTS.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p>
        CBT examines the relationship between thoughts,
        emotions, and behavior. Your first interpretation
        isn't necessarily accurate, even when it feels
        completely convincing.
      </p>

      <p>
        Notice automatic thoughts, identify cognitive
        distortions, and examine the evidence.
        Feeling worthless doesn't establish that
        you are worthless. Feeling certain
        doesn't establish that you're right.
      </p>

      <p>
        Questioning the story you're telling yourself
        can help you respond more deliberately
        instead of treating every thought as a fact.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / DBT -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-yellow"
           aria-labelledby="big-picture-section-6-title">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">

      <p class="big-picture-number">
        06 / DBT
      </p>

      <h2 id="big-picture-section-6-title">
        HOLD<br>
        BOTH.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p>
        DBT brings acceptance and change together.
        Your emotions can make sense, and your
        behavior can still need to change.
        Both things can be true.
      </p>

      <p>
        Wise Mind considers emotions alongside facts.
        Distress tolerance helps you get through
        painful moments without creating additional
        problems. Emotional regulation and
        interpersonal effectiveness support
        relationships, boundaries, and communication.
      </p>

      <p>
        You don't have to deny what you're feeling
        to choose a different response.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 07 / IFS -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-purple"
           aria-labelledby="big-picture-section-7-title">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">

      <p class="big-picture-number">
        07 / IFS
      </p>

      <h2 id="big-picture-section-7-title">
        WHO'S<br>
        TALKING<br>
        RIGHT NOW?
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p>
        IFS explores the needs behind internal conflict.
        Managers seek control, firefighters seek immediate
        relief, and exiles carry vulnerability.
      </p>

      <p>
        Instead of treating every uncomfortable feeling
        or conflicting impulse as an enemy, you can
        approach those patterns with curiosity.
        What is this part trying to protect?
        What does it fear?
      </p>

      <p>
        Understanding an impulse doesn't mean
        you have to follow it. Parts language
        is a framework for reflection,
        not an explanation that replaces
        the medical realities of mania
        or psychosis.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 08 / TAOISM -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-black"
           aria-labelledby="big-picture-section-8-title">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">

      <p class="big-picture-number">
        08 / TAOISM
      </p>

      <h2 id="big-picture-section-8-title">
        STOP<br>
        FORCING<br>
        EVERYTHING.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p>
        Taoism encourages living with change rather
        than fighting every part of it. Yin and yang
        represent complementary qualities, but
        mania and depression aren't spiritual
        opposites that need to balance each other.
        They're medical conditions.
      </p>

      <p>
        Wu wei encourages action without unnecessary
        force. During depression, that may mean
        respecting your limitations instead of
        demanding constant productivity.
        During mania, it may mean allowing
        an idea to exist without immediately
        turning it into a decision.
      </p>

      <p>
        Taoism also encourages humility.
        Feeling certain doesn't mean you're
        correct. Sometimes the most useful
        response is to simplify, slow down,
        and stop trying to control everything.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 09 / PAGANISM -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-pink"
           aria-labelledby="big-picture-section-9-title">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">

      <p class="big-picture-number">
        09 / PAGANISM
      </p>

      <h2 id="big-picture-section-9-title">
        STAY<br>
        FUCKING<br>
        ROOTED.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p>
        Paganism offers many ways to explore your
        relationship with nature, ritual, and
        personal meaning. The changing seasons
        provide a metaphor for responding to
        different circumstances without treating
        bipolar episodes as natural cycles
        that must alternate.
      </p>

      <p>
        A garden needs care, boundaries, and
        sometimes pruning. Similarly, you can
        value your creativity without acting
        on every manic impulse. During depression,
        respecting your limitations and accepting
        support can be more useful than
        forcing yourself to function normally.
      </p>

      <p>
        Ritual, time in nature, and personal
        reflection can remain meaningful parts
        of your life. They don't need to cure
        your illness or turn it into
        a spiritual assignment.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 10 / SHINTO -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-yellow"
           aria-labelledby="big-picture-section-10-title">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">

      <p class="big-picture-number">
        10 / SHINTO
      </p>

      <h2 id="big-picture-section-10-title">
        BEGIN<br>
        AGAIN.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p>
        Shinto emphasizes relationships with nature,
        the kami, and the people and places around you.
        Ritual, gratitude, and purification can
        offer ways to restore connection
        after disruption.
      </p>

      <p>
        Practices such as harae and misogi
        emphasize renewal. They cannot erase
        a manic episode, undo its consequences,
        or replace psychiatric care.
        But the idea of beginning again
        can still hold meaning.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can acknowledge what happened,
        repair what you can, and return
        to familiar routines without
        treating yourself as permanently
        defined by an episode.
      </p>

      <p>
        Harmony doesn't mean never experiencing
        disruption. It means continuing
        to care for your relationships,
        surroundings, and responsibilities.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 11 / PUT THEM TOGETHER -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-purple"
           aria-labelledby="big-picture-section-11-title">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">

      <p class="big-picture-number">
        11 / PUT THEM TOGETHER
      </p>

      <h2 id="big-picture-section-11-title">
        ONE<br>
        FUCKING<br>
        MOMENT.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p>
        When an urge gets loud, an emotion feels
        overwhelming, or your mind starts telling
        you that something needs to happen
        immediately, these perspectives offer
        different ways to respond.
      </p>

      <div class="big-picture-stack">

        <div>
          <span>BUDDHISM</span>
          <p>
            Notice what's happening with compassion.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>HINDUISM</span>
          <p>
            Remember your worth beyond this mental state.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>STOICISM</span>
          <p>
            Work with what you can influence.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>CBT</span>
          <p>
            Check your interpretation against the evidence.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>DBT</span>
          <p>
            Accept the emotion and choose an effective response.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>IFS</span>
          <p>
            Listen for the need behind the urge.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>TAOISM</span>
          <p>
            Stop forcing. Know when to act and when to wait.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>PAGANISM</span>
          <p>
            Stay grounded in your body, boundaries,
            and responsibilities.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>SHINTO</span>
          <p>
            Restore connection and begin again
            without denying what happened.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 12 / COMMON GROUND -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-pink"
           aria-labelledby="big-picture-section-12-title">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">

      <p class="big-picture-number">
        12 / COMMON GROUND
      </p>

      <h2 id="big-picture-section-12-title">
        NOTICE.<br>
        PAUSE.<br>
        CHOOSE.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p>
        These perspectives don't all teach the
        same thing, but several encourage awareness,
        compassion, restraint, and responsibility.
        You can notice an impulse without following it,
        recognize suffering without defining yourself
        by it, and accept your circumstances
        without giving up on change.
      </p>

      <p>
        Compassion includes you and the people
        affected by your actions. Bipolar disorder
        may explain why a decision became difficult
        to manage, but consequences can still
        require attention and repair.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can acknowledge what happened,
        learn from your patterns, establish
        safeguards, and begin again.
        Shame doesn't have to become
        a permanent sentence.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 13 / AND BIPOLAR? -->
  <section class="big-picture-section big-picture-yellow"
           aria-labelledby="big-picture-section-13-title">

    <div class="big-picture-section-heading">

      <p class="big-picture-number">
        13 / AND BIPOLAR?
      </p>

      <h2 id="big-picture-section-13-title">
        A LENS<br>
        ISN'T A<br>
        CURE.
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-copy">

      <p>
        None of these approaches eliminates
        bipolar disorder. Buddhism cannot prevent
        mania through mindfulness. Taoism cannot
        guarantee stability through acceptance.
        Rituals, philosophical insight, and
        coping skills cannot replace medication
        or psychiatric care.
      </p>

      <p>
        These perspectives can support how you
        understand and respond to your experiences.
        They can sit alongside treatment,
        routines, planning, and trusted support.
      </p>

      <p>
        Severe episodes can impair insight
        and judgment. Sometimes you need
        outside help rather than another
        exercise in self-reflection.
        Recognizing that is part of
        caring for yourself.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="big-picture-ending">

    <div>

      <p class="big-picture-ending-small">
        MAYBE THERE ISN'T ONE PERFECT ANSWER.
      </p>

      <h2>
        BUILD YOUR<br>
        OWN FUCKING<br>
        <span>TOOLBOX.</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="big-picture-ending-copy">

      <p>
        The Buddhist Middle Way helps hold
        these perspectives together:
        awareness, compassion, and steady practice.
        You can also borrow from Taoist simplicity,
        Shinto renewal, Pagan connection with nature,
        Stoic responsibility, and the practical
        tools of CBT, DBT, and IFS.
      </p>

      <p>
        You don't need to make every tradition
        agree or turn your suffering into
        something meaningful. Keep what helps
        you live according to your values,
        take responsibility for your actions,
        and care for yourself.
      </p>

      <p>
        You are more than your diagnosis,
        your worst episode, or your most
        convincing thought.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO PERSPECTIVES",
          bipolar_perspectives_path,
          class: "big-picture-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>
